WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100
Good to have you here for first principles daily, episode two.

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:07.700
Today, an industry whose idiot index is begging to be attacked.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.940
Before Ford, a car cost a skilled worker years of wages.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.120
He cut the price by treating it as atoms in motion

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.300
rather than a fitted craft object.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.440
In 1908, a new Model T left the factory priced near $850.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:26.320
By the mid-1920s, the same basic car sold

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.860
for roughly $260.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.700
The drop did not come from cheaper steel or rubber.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.860
It came from refusing to accept the labor hours

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:39.260
and part-by-part fitting that had defined every automobile build before it.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.380
Before Ford, every automobile was assembled in one place

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:47.340
by skilled fitters who filed, drilled, and adjusted each component

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:48.900
until it matched its neighbors.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:53.560
The convention had grown directly from carriage and bicycle practice.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:56.320
Small teams produced low volumes

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.680
and each vehicle was essentially unique.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:03.800
Under that method, a finished car carried the full cost of those fitters time.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:08.360
It also carried the carrying cost of parts waiting for their mates.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:13.500
And it carried the scrap generated when a single misfit ruined an assembly.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:18.260
The price therefore sat far above the value of the raw materials.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.020
The dominant expense was the hours required to reconcile non-interchangeable parts.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:27.440
No one questioned the premise that a car must be built

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.840
the way a fine carriage had always been built.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.240
The assumption was simply inherited.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:38.100
As a result, the industry treated high labor content as an unavoidable feature of the product

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.700
rather than a variable that could be attacked.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.960
The baseline price reflected that inheritance.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:47.100
A machine that only the well-off could purchase,

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.780
even though the steel, wood, rubber, and brass inside it

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:52.180
were ordinary commodities.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:54.880
Ford refused that baseline.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:00.980
He asked what the car would cost if every part arrived ready to bolt or rivet without further fitting.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:06.740
He also asked what the car would cost if the work itself moved past stationary workers

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:08.440
instead of the other way around.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.500
Ford began by insisting on interchangeable parts.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:17.620
This meant the magic wand floor, the cost of the raw materials themselves,

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.840
could finally be approached instead of being buried under fitting labor.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.320
A rough magic wand estimate for the materials in an early model T

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.900
runs on the order of perhaps fifty to eighty dollars in period commodity prices

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.240
for the steel, rubber, brass, and wood.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.140
The finished price of eight hundred fifty dollars,

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:39.940
therefore implied an idiot index on the order of ten to seventeen

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.100
before any redesign began.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.420
That ratio announced that the dominant cost was not the atoms

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.000
but the hours spent reconciling them.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.280
Interchangeability attacked the ratio directly by forcing every supplier

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:59.580
and every department to hold tolerances that had previously been left to the fitters file.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.640
Once parts could be drawn from bins at random and still assemble,

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:07.660
the next move was to bring the work to the worker on a moving line.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.380
The line eliminated the time each chassis spent waiting

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:14.780
while fitters walked between benches or search for the next part.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:19.960
Early experiments showed assembly time falling from roughly twelve hours per car

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.040
to a few hours.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:23.880
Later refinements pushed it lower still.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:29.360
Vertical integration followed because Ford calculated that the cost of motion

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.380
included every shipment and every markup between foundry and assembly.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:37.860
By owning the steel mill, the glass plant, and the rubber plantations,

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:42.080
he removed the intermediate handling steps whose costs had been passed along

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:43.260
in the finished price.

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:48.140
Each of these moves was chosen because it reduced the labor multiplier

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:50.900
that sat between raw material and finished car.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:53.120
The trade-off was rigidity.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:57.040
The line only worked once the product itself stopped changing.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:02.340
Ford accepted that constraint and froze the Model T design for 19 years

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:05.480
so the process could be driven toward its physical limit.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.480
The idiot index fell because the numerator, total cost,

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.840
dropped faster than any change in the material denominator.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:16.060
The price trajectory is well documented.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.560
It runs from the initial $850 down through successive cuts

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:24.480
to the $260 range by 1925.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.100
Part counts and assembly hours fell in parallel,

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.400
though exact contemporary figures vary by source.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.700
The new idiot index was still well above one.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.980
Steel, rubber, and fuel remained real costs.

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:41.940
The line itself required expensive tooling and supervision.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.620
The human trade-off was equally real.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:47.880
The pace and monotony of the line produced high turnover

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.180
until Ford countered with the famous $5 day.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.600
Even after the wage increase,

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.180
the fundamental gap between material floor and finished price

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:01.140
had narrowed dramatically compared with the pre-Fordcraft baseline.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:04.480
What remained hard was any further reduction

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.040
once the design was locked.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:09.300
Later competitors would have to rethink the car itself,

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:10.840
not merely its assembly.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:15.240
A part or assembly whose price greatly exceeds its materials

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.260
is announcing a design or process problem,

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:19.780
not a shortage of atoms.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:23.380
Question the inherited requirement, hand-fitting,

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:26.320
stationary assembly, fragmented supply

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.480
before optimizing within it.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:33.660
The same logic that turned an $850 car into a $260 car

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:36.860
is available wherever the idiot index remains high

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:40.020
because the process still copies yesterday's convention

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.340
rather than the physics of the materials.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.220
Who will apply it next and to what product

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:47.240
whose price today still looks inevitable?

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:49.680
That's First Principles Daily for Today.

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:52.280
If this changed how you see what things cost,

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:54.200
share it with someone who depreciated

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.060
and subscribe wherever you listen.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:57.820
I'm Patrick and Vancouver,

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.600
one example or one opportunity every day.

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:01.520
See you tomorrow.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.460
This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:08.320
Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
